    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A sliding fault in brittle rock may induce a local near-tip concentration of tensile stresses sufficient in magnitude to form a dominantly opening-mode wing crack. These features may affect fracture connectivity and local permeability in fault zones, impacting various engineering tasks such as the production of hydrocarbon reservoirs and mitigation of groundwater contamination. Therefore, accurate numerical modeling of wing crack initiation and propagation is an important tool for understanding the impacts of wing crack formation in a rock body. This paper briefly reviews boundary element methods (BEM) for simulating incipient wing crack growth, and theoretical models for kink angle prediction. Other numerical tools, such as complementarity and cohesive end zones, help to fine-tune the displacement discontinuity BEM and simulate more realistic propagation paths. Additional field data will help to constrain the way in which wing crack initiation and propagation is modeled.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
